SM(465,{"n":"Acts 13","a":"Fr Stephen De Young Archives/Acts 13.mp3","d":2872.7,"s":[[0.16,"Come and study holy scriptures with us as Father Stephen DeYoung teaches verse by verse on the podcast The Whole Counsel of God."],[8.08,"Father Stephen is a former Protestant pastor and Bible teacher in the Reformed Church, but now an Orthodox priest serving at Archangel Gabriel Orthodox Church in Lafayette, Louisiana."],[21.76,"So we'll get started here in uh just a minute, and when we do get started, we're starting at the beginning of uh Acts the Apostles chapter 13."],[30.56,"Um as I always say, by the time people are hearing this uh on the uh internet, the first Bible study on the book of Acts will be available there too, so you can hear my introduction to the book there."],[45.6,"Uh to give you an idea of the lag time."],[48.56,"Today they posted uh the end of John chapter 17."],[52.88,"Uh-huh. Wow. So we're a little bit ahead of where the the uh podcast is on internet."],[63.64,"But uh at any rate, um to get us caught up uh to where we are now."],[70.2,"Um last time uh we were talking about really the beginnings of the church in Antioch, uh, where uh Saints Paul and Barnabas were ministering."],[81.8,"Uh and uh we heard uh some about the further persecution of the church uh in Jerusalem under Herod, that Herod had killed uh Saint James, the son of Zebedee, St."],[94.68,"John's brother, um, and uh then had sort of gotten his comeuppance shortly thereafter uh when he was eaten by worms and died, we are told."],[109.44,"In that order."],[131.76,"Beginning with the Jewish authorities in Jerusalem, and now also Herod, and now also others."],[137.36,"But every time they attack the church and persecute it, they end up just spreading the church out more and the church grows more."],[149.4,"So there's a there's a famous saying from later in church history describing all of the martyrdom in the Roman Empire before Saint Constantine, that the blood of the martyrs was the seed of the church."],[160.76,"That sort of, you know, as much as they tried to destroy it, it just blossomed into new life and and uh growth."],[171.64,"And so uh as we pick up here uh again, we also read about uh after Herod had had killed St."],[178.68,"James, you read about Herod's attempt to kill Saint Peter, that didn't go as well as he had hoped."],[185.16,"Uh, since even though he had St."],[186.84,"Peter under heavy guard in the middle of the barracks, uh an angel came and let him loose, and so St."],[193.8,"Peter got away."],[195.84,"And now from that in Jerusalem, we're now going to turn our attention here in chapter 13 back to Antioch in Syria."],[204.8,"The reason I reiterate Antioch in Syria is that within this very chapter, we're going to run into another Antioch, another place."],[223.04,"That was one of the dynasties that succeeded after Alexander the Great that ruled Syria."],[231.68,"And that Syria, of course, included what's now Lebanon and what's now Jordan, and at that time included even Judea, and up into what's now Turkey, the eastern part of what's now Turkey, was all controlled by the Seleucids and over into Afghanistan."],[250.36,"I mean, all the way across Iraq and Iran and all of that."],[253.88,"But so those rulers, since Antiochus was sort of their family name, there were six Antiochuses in that dynasty, they tended to name cities after themselves and after their sons and after their fathers."],[266.84,"And so you end up with a lot of cities named Antioch, sort of the same way you end up with a lot of Alexandria's named after Alexander the Great, and a lot of Caesarea's named after Caesar."],[278.44,"So uh this Antioch is is the is the capital of the eastern part of the Roman Empire and is the third biggest city in uh the Roman world after uh Rome and Alexandria."],[295.04,"Uh so it is a major capital at this point, um as opposed to the other Antiochs that we'll see there are smaller cities."],[305.36,"So, unless anybody has any questions or comments or insinuations or anything else, we'll go ahead and get started in uh Acts the Apostles chapter 13, verse 1."],[322.8,"Now, in the church that was at Antioch, there were certain prophets and teachers, Barnabas, Simeon, who was called Niger, Lucius of Cyrene, Menaean, who had been brought up with Herod, the Tetrarch, and Saul."],[344.52,"So we mentioned before that the Herod who we were just talking about, the Herod who just died, was not Herod the Great, who was the first Herod."],[353.74,"That's the Herod who murdered the infants at the time Jesus was born."],[358.94,"And he died shortly thereafter."],[362.94,"Because the Romans were not real happy with him when he died, they chopped up his territory into four pieces."],[371.34,"And his four son, four of his sons, not his horses, but four. of his sons inherited pieces"],[377.78,"And so this Herod, who we just read about who died, is referred to as Herod the Tetrarch."],[383.14,"Tetrarch, Ark is ruler, like Arch, like we have archbishops."],[389.38,"And the Tetra is four."],[392.02,"So he's the ruler of a fourth, ruler of a quarter, basically, of his father's territory."],[398.98,"So uh this uh Monothan who we read about here uh is someone who obviously was from some kind of wealthy family, somehow connected to the to the Herods, and had so had grown up alongside Herod when he was young."],[416.58,"Uh now notice also we saw uh Saint Agabus, I don't remember if it was last time or the time before."],[419.06,"I think it was last time, who was described as a prophet"],[426.06,"Remember, we read that certain prophets had come from Jerusalem."],[429.26,"We talked about how prophet didn't mean they were sort of predictors of, but that they were sort of teachers in the Old Testament sense."],[437.74,"And you notice here that these people, Saint Barnabas, Saul, who is Saint Paul, and Simeon and Manon, are described here as prophets and teachers."],[449.18,"So they're included in this group."],[453.18,"So these are people who are preaching the faith, preaching Christ."],[457.5,"Do we hear any more about the names other than Barnabas and Paul?"],[462.22,"No. That's no."],[464.46,"We mentioned before, and you see this a lot in St"],[472.1,"Luke's Gospel, where you will see just sort of names pop up of people."],[478.34,"Like, you know, uh Simon of Cyrene's Cyrene, whose sons were, you know, Rufus and and it's you're sort of like, well, why is that?"],[489.46,"Well, that's there because at the time this was written, you know, these are actual people."],[493.86,"There are people who would so you could go and you know, if and talk to them."],[500.08,"Say, is this true?"],[501.76,"Or it's also, you know, in many cases, St."],[504.16,"Luke said at the beginning of his gospel that he had sort of gathered, you talked to eyewitnesses and sort of gathered people's testimony to put together the story because he wasn't there with Jesus along the way for most of his life."],[517.72,"Um, and so he had talked to other people."],[519.88,"And so, in many cases, these names he's listing are people he talked to."],[523.97,"So he may not have been able to talk to Simon of Cyrene, but he talked to his sons who told him about, yeah, we were in Jerusalem at that time, and they made our father, you know, carrying Jesus' cross, where he got some of this information."],[537.41,"So that's why sometimes we get these names of sort of, and you're sort of like, Well, who's that?"],[542.77,"You know. These are people who were significant people of the church at that time, you know, and so St."],[549.89,"Luke is reporting that to indicate, you know, hey, there',s there this'.s this person who was there person who is there"],[557.45,"Um as we also mentioned Saint Luke in both his gospel and in the book of Acts is writing in what was at the time a historical style."],[566.73,"He's kind of deliberately aping, in fact, Thucydides, the Greek historian, um, sort of in that style, to because he's he's one of his emphases is that these are all things that really happened."],[580.43,"He's not just telling you a story, it's not mythology, you know, it's not a bunch of folk tales that he collected, you know, but these are actual people, actual events."],[591.31,"Here's what happened, and how it happened, and how this all unfolded."],[596.35,"Um, I mentioned I also mentioned that at this point, in terms of people he talked to, people he did research with, because we're gonna get to a point here in just a little bit in the book of Acts where all of a sudden, instead of saying they, it's gonna say we."],[611.75,"Because at a certain point, St."],[613.99,"Luke is gonna be traveling with St."],[616.95,"Paul. And so once he's traveling with them, he switches to saying we."],[621.75,"We went here, we traveled there."],[624.87,"So we're gonna get to the point where we're at things that he was an eyewitness to."],[630.31,"But in this earlier part, you know, these are events where he wasn't there, so he's getting it from other people."],[636.95,"So he collected FedEx and he's yeah, and spoke to different people."],[642.23,"Isn't he one of the people that oh gosh?"],[643.27,"Emmaus Traditionally, yeah, he's the other unnamed disciple there."],[654.37,"Yeah. So that's why I said he wasn't there for most of Jesus."],[660.13,"Earthly life. He was probably there for parts."],[664.69,"Well, or there at the end in Jerusalem."],[669.65,"But so this is also related to our understanding from tradition that St."],[675.73,"Luke was the first iconographer, that he painted icons of the Theotokos, um who was one of the people whom he talked to."],[685.69,"Which is how he knew about the things and in Luke 1 2."],[690.73,"About the the Archangel Gabriel coming to coming to Mary and and the birth of Jesus and everything because she could tell him about it because he talked to her and he was a doctor."],[705.93,"He was he was a physician, yeah."],[708.73,"At at the time."],[709.45,"It was a little different than doctors today."],[711.45,"Yeah. But he was yeah, he was he was a physician."],[715.45,"He helped sick people that's yeah."],[717.85,"I mean that first icon."],[725.77,"Okay. So these are the four primary people who are leading the Christian community there in Antioch."],[735.73,"As we read uh earlier also, uh this it's now correct to call this the Christian community in Antioch because in Antioch they had started calling them Christians."],[745.65,"Yes. They actually had that title at this point."],[754.93,"As they ministered to the Lord and fasted, the Holy Spirit said, Now separate to me Barnabas and Saul for the work to which I have called them."],[766.29,"Then having fasted and prayed and laid hands on them, they sent them away."],[771.97,"So they looked together as a community."],[774.37,"Notice uh it says, as they ministered to to the the Lord."],[777.81,"the We use word ministered to talk about ministering to people."],[780.49,"That's not the way we normally use the word ministered."],[786.87,"Like I, as the priest of the parish, I minister to the people."],[790.49,"Of the parish."],[791.77,"And that's right."],[792.73,"What it's describing is worship."],[794.89,"The word ministered there is really referring to the type of service, frankly, that they did at the temple."],[802.78,"So strictly speaking, it's referring to sacrificial worship."],[807.44,"That they ministered to the Lord."],[809.28,"In this case, this would have been the Eucharist."],[812.42,"Not but it wasn't what we know today as the it's not what we do at church now."],[819.54,"Yes, it is."],[820.5,"It is, yes, it wasn't this from that early times."],[820.9,"Yes Now that now at this point, there are still what we now call the liturgy, was at this point still two separate services."],[835.18,"Because uh at this point, there isn't sort of Judaism over here and Christianity over here."],[842.56,"It's these two separate religions."],[844.96,"Uh so uh people would go to the synagogues, we're gonna see St."],[849.84,"Paul, every time he goes to a city, he goes first to the synagogue on Saturday, and there they would read scripture and preach."],[858.56,"You know, now that now they're preaching about Christ."],[861.57,"But uh they're reading the scripture, the Old Testament at this point, because the New Testament isn't written yet."],[867.37,"And uh and uh and preaching on that."],[870.49,"So that's the first part of what we do in liturgy."],[873.37,"We come in and we sing psalms and then read scripture and then preach, and they did the same thing."],[879.37,"Um now again they didn't have the New Testament yet."],[881.77,"So like when I come out with the gospel book for the little entrance, they had a procession with the Torah scroll."],[888.09,"Oh they had the Torah, the Pedateuch."],[891.45,"Just to say the five books of Locke."],[893.29,"And they still do."],[893.85,"If you go to an Orthodox synagogue today, they still do."],[896.81,"The Torah being in the first five books of the Old Testament."],[899.69,"That's right. And we do that with the gospel book now."],[903.37,"The gospel. Matthew, Martin, and John."],[905.37,"Okay. So, but it was basically the same idea."],[908.97,"That was on Saturday."],[909.53,"So Saturday And then on Sunday, just the Christians would get together to celebrate the Eucharist."],[920.21,"There was two separate pieces."],[921.81,"And then what happened is at the between the beginning and the middle of the second century, more toward the middle of the second century, the Jews ex threw the Christians out of the synagogue."],[938.37,"They declared that the Christians were heretics."],[940.61,"They're not Jewish anymore."],[944.13,"And so the Christians couldn't go to the synagogue service anymore."],[949.33,"And so they took that service and they moved that to Sunday also."],[953.41,"And that's why we now, still to this day of the Orthodox Church, we do the whole thing on Sunday now"],[954.73,"Okay. We do the first part, which is called the Liturgy of the Word, where we sing Psalms and read the scriptures and preach, and then we go right into celebrating the Eucharist."],[971.69,"We just do both together."],[976.33,"Yeah, if you read in Saint Justin Martyr, who wrote in the middle part of the second century, and he's writing right after this happened."],[983.69,"So, for example, one of his works is called The Dialogue with Trifo the Jew."],[987.45,"Trifo is a Jewish person who he knows, who he's having a sort of sometimes friendly debate about religion with."],[996.73,"And he's talking about these things."],[998.09,"He's talking about the fact that the Christians had just sort of been expelled."],[999.69,"But he also describes Christian worship"],[1004.85,"And if you look, it's exactly an outline of what we do on Sunday."],[1012.85,"So this is this is the Eucharist part."],[1016.77,"That's just the Christians."],[1018.9,"On uh on Sunday, on the Lord's Day, the day that Jesus rose from the dead."],[1027.62,"Oh, okay. At that early time."],[1029.78,"Yep. Yeah. And and we're good, we're gonna read."],[1033.62,"Um, this is only a few years uh before St."],[1038.34,"Paul writes First Corinthians, and we're gonna read in First Corinthians."],[1041.54,"St. Paul's just gonna describe exactly how they do the Eucharist."],[1044.1,"He's gonna say, What I received I also hand it on to you"],[1050.06,"And he describes how he taught them to."],[1053.26,"So yeah, this is from the very beginning."],[1058.54,"It says you'll worship, that's why I say worship."],[1060.86,"Okay, yeah. And I was wondering how they worship."],[1063.66,"Yeah, this English translation says ministered to the Lord."],[1067.04,"And that's actually a better translation."],[1071.04,"That's that they were worshiping, they were offering worship to the Lord."],[1081.76,"Yeah. That's what it is."],[1083.2,"Well, and it's close, it's closer to the original languages of the Bible too."],[1087.84,"English is a lot. further removed from Greek, it definitely from Hebrew and Aramaic"],[1093.4,"It's much farther away than Arabic is."],[1102.52,"So there are uh they're ministering ministering to the Lord, meaning they're worshiping."],[1108.94,"And they're fasting together."],[1111.9,"And the Holy Spirit tells them that that they need that uh there's something else that uh Barnabas and Saul need to do."],[1123.52,"We know they've been there for more than a year."],[1126.48,"right Ministering there."],[1128.5,"The Holy Spirit needs them to do something else."],[1130.95,"So we have the other two who will continue on here in Antioch."],[1132.15,"But Barnabas and Saul have something else to do"],[1137.31,"And so they fast and pray and they set them apart for this special mission that's about to begin."],[1145.23,"Which they don't quite know what it is yet."],[1147.31,"No, they haven't been told what it is."],[1148.99,"It's just you need to let them move on now, is what they're told."],[1158.19,"Yeah. Now his his name it's gonna sort of he's gonna start being referred to as Paul and stop being referred to as Saul."],[1168.67,"But that's not like um St."],[1171.31,"Peter, where you know Christ gave him this sort of nickname."],[1176.87,"Or like Abram and Abraham."],[1176.91,"Right. There's not a formal thing like that that happens"],[1182.97,"It's that it's that it it's sort of it's the same thing we were talking about last time when we talked about Saint John Mark."],[1190.95,"That that the name John is really Jonah."],[1195.16,"Brought over into Greek."],[1197.56,"Um and that was his Hebrew name, but Romans and Greeks had a lot of trouble pronouncing Hebrew names because there are a lot of sounds they didn't use."],[1210.28,"Um and so they would um they would often have Greek or a Greek or a Latin name, like in the case of St."],[1221.76,"John Mark, Mark isn't his last name, it's his Latin name, Marcus."],[1227.44,"So they would have something to call him."],[1229.92,"Even Saint Peter."],[1231.66,"We always refer to him as Saint Peter, Petros."],[1234.54,"That's the Greek form of his name."],[1237.39,"Yeah. His name was actually his name was actually Caphas."],[1243.15,"It's sometimes done Cephas."],[1245.29,"Which is Aramaic for rock."],[1249.93,"So and we're gonna see Saint Paul refers to him as Cephas later on in his in his letters, because that's what he was actually called in Aramaic by, you know, the people."],[1261.21,"So it's the same thing with Saul and Paul."],[1264.81,"ul. Uh Sa, we we know from his letters, we're gonna see, we're gonna read later, uh, that St"],[1271.51,"Paul was from the tribe of Benjamin, which is the tribe that King Saul in the Old Testament was from."],[1277.03,"So Saul was probably a popular name in that uh in that tribe."],[1284.31,"So uh so Saul is sort of his Hebrew name, but we know he was from Tarsus, which was a fairly major Roman port city."],[1292.55,"Uh and so there were a lot of dealings there with people, Greeks and Romans and that kind of thing, who didn't speak any Aramaic and didn't know that."],[1301.27,"And so Paulos is a is a Greek name, yeah."],[1306.99,"Which comes up Bulos or yeah."],[1308.63,"And some names are completely different"],[1309.63,"Becomes Pavel in the Eastern Europe."],[1315.23,"We pronounce it in Arabic way much different than English."],[1318.27,"It is not even related."],[1320.27,"Well Cephas and Petros are pretty different."],[1328.99,"But so yeah, so in this context he's still with he's when he's in the Jewish community was in Jerusalem when he's with the Jewish community in Antioch, the Jewish community of Adrian, they tend to call him Saul."],[1343.07,"We're gonna see he's gonna be traveling to Gentile cities, to Greek cities, and so people are going to be calling him Paul."],[1353.07,"So it's the same person."],[1354.39,"So is is Paul a Greek or presumably Greek name that was in general circulation?"],[1363.35,"I mean, where there are other people named that."],[1365.83,"Not really. It was probably mostly trying to come up with something close to Saul that they would have, they would have less trouble with."],[1376.55,"Yeah. So there's there's a lot of that that happens in the ancient world."],[1381.35,"Um for example, the the Philistines, um, which is where Palestine gets its name."],[1390.63,"But the Philistines, their actual name, they were part of a an invasion."],[1396.07,"They were actually from Crete, from what's now Crete."],[1399.47,"But they they tried to invade, they with a group of uh of other tribes called the Sea Peoples, tried to invade Egypt and failed."],[1408.27,"But they set up colonies sort of along the coast near Egypt."],[1412.59,"Ashkelon and Ashdod and Gaza and yeah."],[1415.71,"And so, but that their name was actually, we only know the consonants, we don't know the vowels, but the consonants were P R S T."],[1425.31,"So it's probably something like the Perest."],[1428.21,"But apparently the Egyptians at that time had kind of a lisp and couldn't do Rs."],[1432.93,"Uh-huh. So they called them the Pelest."],[1436.37,"And that's where Philistine ended up and Palestine ended up coming from because Egyptians couldn't pronounce Rs."],[1442.93,"They pronounced them like L's."],[1443.73,"So there was a lot of that just with different languages and different with modern names"],[1449.61,"I mean, you find a different form of common names in each European country."],[1454.25,"Oh, yeah, yeah."],[1454.89,"And I think I I think I mentioned last time a lot of Asian people I know in this country whose names us white people find hard to pronounce will just have a channel."],[1466.89,"Just have an American name where they say, Yeah, people just call me."],[1470.33,"You know. And other places too."],[1472.41,"Um I had a prisoner in another church from Ethiopia whose name was Abanish, and people had trouble pronouncing it, so everybody just called her Abby."],[1482.01,"Because it was sort of easy for Americans."],[1489.17,"So it's a it's a similar kind of thing with Paul."],[1495.65,"So being sent out by the Holy Spirit, they went down to Seleucia, and from there they sailed to Cyprus."],[1502.37,"And when they arrived in Salamis, they preached the word of God in the synagogues of the Jews."],[1509.57,"They also had John as their assistant."],[1512.05,"Now, this John, remember, is Saint John Mark, yeah, who we know as Mark, the author of St."],[1518.45,"Mark's Gospel. We met him last time."],[1521.49,"And remember, he went back with St."],[1524.69,"Barnabas and St."],[1525.65,"Paul. He went back to Antioch with them."],[1527.49,"And now this is telling us they brought him along with them when they went on this journey."],[1531.49,"He's still quite young."],[1532.77,"Yeah. Now, if you wonder why they went to Cyprus first, remember that's where St."],[1538.25,"Barnabas is from."],[1539.53,"Oh. Remember, he had owned these estates there and had been converted in Jerusalem."],[1545.2,"And when that happened, he had sold his estates in Cyprus and just sort of stayed in Jerusalem."],[1550.4,"So when they set out, they go back to where Saint Barnabas is from."],[1556.16,"He knows lots of people there."],[1557.86,"And preach there."],[1558.74,"And notice again, they're preaching in this, they're going to the synagogues."],[1562.12,"Remember when it says preach the word."],[1564.62,"Word isn't the Bible yet."],[1566.88,"Word is Jesus."],[1568.01,"Word is Christ."],[1569.29,"So they're going to the synagogues and they're proclaiming, hey, the Messiah has come, it's Jesus of Nazareth."],[1575.85,"Here's what he did"],[1576.29,"Here's who he is."],[1579.89,"And they're proclaiming that in the synagogues."],[1589.57,"And they start in uh Salamis, which is one of the major cities in Cyprus at this time."],[1599.17,"So we're about to read they're going to go other places, but they start sort of in this major port city and then work from there."],[1606.93,"And when they had gone through the island in Paphos, they found a certain sorcerer, a false prophet, a Jew whose name was Bar Jesus."],[1619.09,"Okay. Now, Bar. Jesus is a pretty bad transliteration"],[1624.73,"Because of course, Bar is son of in Aramaic."],[1628.65,"And right, it's Joshua."],[1630.41,"So his father's name was Joshua, Yeshua."],[1633.69,"Which again, Joshua in the Old Testament, there's lots of people named that."],[1640.57,"No relation to Jesus of Nazareth."],[1643.53,"Yeah. So this is just some of the things you can do with that."],[1647.45,"Yeah. But so while they're they're going around from city to city, sort of all around the island of Cyprus, and they run into this person who's a sorcerer."],[1658.57,"But notice this sorcerer is a Jewish person."],[1662.19,"This isn't a Gentile, this isn't a a Roman or Greek doing sort of their magic or practicing their paganism."],[1674.79,"This may seem odd, you know, because we have this impression."],[1680.55,"We tend to, I mentioned this in the past, but we tend to take sort of Judaism the way it exists today in the world, you know, rabbinic Judaism, and we assume that that's what Judaism was like all the way back."],[1692.71,"And that's not true."],[1695.13,"Rabbinic Judaism is uh Judaism as we know it now is literally a different religion."],[1700.66,"That comes into being really in the really in the 5th and 6th centuries, as the Talmud starts getting sort of brought together and finalized."],[1709.14,"So we've found even in Galilee, even in Galilee, not that far from Jerusalem, in the first century synagogues there, there are five first century synagogues that have been excavated in Galilee."],[1721.9,"And a lot of them have like zodiacs in the floor mosaics."],[1727.5,"They have sort of all of this."],[1730.38,"One of them has uh Medusa heads on the outside, which the Greeks believed would sort of scare off evil spirits and bad luck."],[1743.18,"So even that close to Jerusalem, there were a lot of Jews who had a lot of superstit pagan superstitions and things they'd picked up."],[1753.02,"Well, this is this goes all the way back because the Old Testament prophets are always worried about these pagan practices."],[1761.64,"Right. Um so there there was sort of this smooshing."],[1766.76,"And the further you got away from Jerusalem, sort of the less pure your Judaism got."],[1773.48,"Because these were people who were, you know, a lot of these people, unless they were very wealthy, would live and die their whole lives without ever seeing Jerusalem."],[1781.64,"Without ever participating in a sacrifice."],[1784.53,"All they had for their religion was they'd get together on Saturday and pray at the synagogue."],[1790.93,"Because it was a really expensive to travel."],[1795.65,"Yeah. And so they lived their whole lives basically in a pagan world."],[1800.81,"And so a lot of at least six days a week they were pa you know living in a pagan world."],[1806.25,"Some of them probably all seven."],[1811.69,"Very much like Jews today."],[1813.85,"Well, not just Jews today."],[1815.21,"I mean a lot of us today."],[1818.09,"That's I mean there is that tendency, you know, we we live in w well, we definitely live in a culture that's not orthodox and you know, that's ig increasingly becoming non and even anti-Christian."],[1832.33,"You know? And so there's there's a temptation with that to sort of, you know, well, on Sunday, I'm gonna go and be a Christian, and then the rest of the week I'm gonna sort of do what I gotta do. at my job and my career and school and this and that and the other, you know"],[1848.85,"You know, so I mean it's a temptation for us too."],[1851.57,"But and also a lot of them, just like a lot of us, gave in to that temptation, you know, and this is one of them."],[1858.47,"Who had had uh gotten involved in and and this was this was a way to make a living."],[1865.45,"This wasn't just sort of some weird stuff, weird religious stuff he got into."],[1869.53,"This was a way to make a living."],[1871.85,"Uh doing astrological things for people, interpreting dreams, interpreting omens, making potions and stuff, you know, that would make people fall in love with you, or you know, give you energy, or you know, um, that kind of stuff isn't new."],[1887.77,"That happened then too"],[1888.29,"Snake oil salesmen are sort of a new a new thing."],[1894.51,"Common in Judaism that there would be people like that, or is this it was not common in Judaism, but it was very common in the pagan world."],[1900.83,"Yeah. And so this is just a Jewish person who's sort of blended into his pagan environment."],[1909.79,"Um, and may or may not have been still showing up at synagogue on Saturday, but the rest of the week is basically living, living as a pagan."],[1922.91,"Okay. Dinosaurus is an A"],[1927.31,"Yeah. Poor Jesus."],[1929.39,"Um, who was with the proconsul, Sergiusus Paul, an intelligent man."],[1932.43,"This man called for Barnabas and Saul and sought to hear the word of God"],[1940.15,"Okay, so he had some kind of connection to the local proconsul who was part of the local, local representative of the Roman government."],[1950.09,"And so through him, uh, and since he had found out about Saul and Barnabas, even though it doesn't say so, it's probably a good guess that uh he was showing up in a synagogue at least once in a while."],[1967.29,"Because that's probably where he would have run into them."],[1969.61,"Because as we've seen, they're going right now and preaching in the synagogues."],[1974.01,"But we don't know for sure."],[1975.13,"But he tells this pro-onscul, hey, you know, there are these two going around preaching this new thing."],[1983.37,"You ought to, it's interesting, you ought to hear it."],[1986.17,"Now that may seem strange to us."],[1987.93,"We have to remember they don't have television in this day and age."],[1993.77,"Don't have smartphones, don't have YouTube, don't have newspapers, don't have, you know, most people don't even no nobody has many books."],[2002.83,"Even the proconsul, you know, he may have had a few books, but there's nobody who has their own personal library."],[2009.97,"If you live in Alexandria, you might maybe, and you're wealthy, you might have access to the ones there."],[2016.99,"And so um this is, you know, the the the things that were done for entertainment at this time were you would go and see plays."],[2027.29,"Performances, you would go and see uh athletic competitions and gladiatorial games."],[2035.34,"And you would go to hear orations."],[2038.14,"People would speak."],[2039.58,"Um, and there was a big emphasis on that speaking part."],[2044.14,"So if someone was a good speaker, there there were whole schools of rhetoric at this time, and there were, you know, Aristotle had written treatises on rhetoric centuries before this."],[2054.22,"So this was an established kind of thing."],[2056.54,"And so if someone was a good speaker, it didn't even really matter so much what they talked about."],[2062.38,"You know. it was just if this person was good, it was it was entertaining, and so people would come to hear them speak"],[2068.74,"And so that's why it's not that we shouldn't think here that that Mar Jesus is necessarily this super sincere convert who's trying to get the proconsul saved or something."],[2079.94,"It's it's more, hey, you know, these these guys are pretty impressive speakers."],[2084.9,"You might find this, you know, interesting, diverting, entertaining."],[2089.7,"The same way it's sort of like if we saw a TV show that we really enjoyed, we might say to someone, hey, you should watch this show, you know, it's really good."],[2099.22,"You know, so it's sort of at uh at that level, and that that actually continued for some time, it's only recently. that our technology, beginning with the radio, sort of destroyed that"],[2113.5,"But I mean, even in, you know, uh talk of lecturers in 19th century America."],[2120.46,"Yeah, I was going to say in the 18th and 19th century, you know."],[2122.54,"I mean, George Whitfield, you know, there were these famous preachers at the end of the 18th and beginning of the 19th centuries who would just travel from place to place and preach."],[2131.82,"And they would preach for hours."],[2133.82,"You know, and people would go."],[2135.26,"I mean, there are people who are interested in different levels."],[2137.58,"One of the funny things in Ben Franklin's autobiography is he went to hear George Whitfield speak."],[2143.18,"And uh basically the thing that he he wasn't really paying attention to what he was saying."],[2147.9,"He was just fascinated again at how well the guy could project his voice."],[2151.18,"And so he just started pacing off to see how far away he could get and still hear him."],[2153.26,"And it was a couple of blocks"],[2157.54,"So apparently he could really, yeah, he could really project."],[2161.54,"But uh but so even as recently as that, you know, just someone who was good at oration could draw a crowd, you know."],[2171.06,"Um so we're a little disassociated from that now with all of our entertainment."],[2176.74,"But um that's what it was like in the ancient world."],[2182.66,"And so we're also told that that this particular proconsul is an intelligent man."],[2188.26,"What that really means is that he's he's an educated person, meaning he's interested in this kind of thing."],[2195.54,"There are a lot of people who are sort of not curious and interested in this kind of thing."],[2204.54,"But he was."],[2235.58,"Are the same person."],[2237.98,"So his name was Anime."],[2239.58,"Anime like things"],[2242.3,"Yeah. That's the translation."],[2243.34,"Bar Jesus, of course, is basically he's the son of his dad"],[2243.86,"Yeah. You know?"],[2250.9,"Whereas Alevis is what he was what he was what he would have been called himself."],[2259.46,"And so he isn't sold."],[2263.86,"As interesting as he found Saint Paul and Saint Bartemas, he's not he's not converting."],[2273.59,"And when he detects that the pro-council might be being swayed by what they have to say, he sort of argues the counterpoint."],[2281.29,"You know, well now, you know."],[2286.81,"Then Saul, who also is called Paul."],[2289.89,"Is this the first time that this is the first time that we're told this, yes."],[2294.13,"Filled with the Holy Spirit, looked intently at him and said, Oh full of all deceit and all fraud, you son of the devil, you enemy of all righteousness, will you not cease perverting the straight ways of the Lord?"],[2309.73,"And now indeed the hand of the Lord is upon you, and you shall be blind, not seeing the sun for a time."],[2318.69,"So that's a little harsh."],[2323.25,"Yeah. We have to um I think I commented on this a couple of times when we were going through Saint John's Gospel when uh Christ spoke to the Pharisees."],[2334.06,"You know, we read him calling them a brood of vipers and hypocrites and woe to you and this stuff."],[2338.78,"And it's sort of like, wow, Jesus is being really unchrist-like."],[2343.34,"He's being so mean, you know."],[2346.06,"Um, and you can say the same thing here with Saul."],[2350.51,"It's kind of harsh."],[2351.87,"But um part of that is because uh especially our modern modern culture."],[2358.68,"Our current culture, we're very concerned about offending people."],[2364.12,"And we're often very easily offended ourselves."],[2368.48,"And there was more concerned at this time in history with telling the truth than with worrying about offending people."],[2378.56,"And the reason for that was the reason for that was what could cause someone to change, what could cause someone to change their mind, to change their thinking, to repent, is the truth."],[2392.06,"Is the truth."],[2409.19,"Just let him wander off and go to hell."],[2411.85,"You know, that's not loving."],[2414.67,"If someone is doing evil, if someone is wicked, if someone is destroying them.ing themselves spiritually, then the most loving th you can do for them is tell that"],[2424.63,"Right? Is tell them you're lying to yourself."],[2428.36,"You're doing evil."],[2430.38,"You need to repent and do good."],[2433.02,"Because if they do that, then that is the best thing that can happen to them."],[2438.7,"And so contrary to our instinct when we first read this, what St."],[2445.34,"Paul is doing here is trying, albeit in a harsh way, because sometimes harshness is what you need."],[2453.55,"To get this person to repent."],[2456.69,"To get this person to repent and see the truth."],[2459.81,"Um, if he didn't love him, he wouldn't have said anything"],[2464.25,"He would have just ignored him and let him thought that's law."],[2469.45,"Yes. Apparently the blindness isn't permanent."],[2474.41,"No. Not seeing the sun that's on."],[2477.21,"Uh well, and notice what specifically."],[2482.06,"In addition to just telling him this, St."],[2484.46,"Paul gives him this sign."],[2485.74,"You're gonna be blind for a little while to show that look, what I'm telling you is true."],[2490.59,"This isn't just me disagreeing with you, and we have a disagreement and we're don't like each other."],[2496.4,"But this is me."],[2498.18,"This is me uh giving you this sign."],[2502.26,"So you know it's it's the truth."],[2503.54,"If they say it and it doesn't work, then it kind of backfire sign"],[2505.02,"Well, it better work if you say it."],[2510.14,"But notice this is what?"],[2512.54,"This is exactly what happened to him just a few chapters ago."],[2517.82,"Remember? Why? And why did it happen to him?"],[2522.14,"Because he was going down the wrong path."],[2523.98,"He was persecuting Christians."],[2526.14,"He was opposing Christ."],[2530.38,"And when Christ revealed himself to him and got knocked off, there was he got struck blind."],[2534.44,"That caused him to turn his life around."],[2538.42,"So Saint Paul is trying to do the same thing for this Elemus for this bar Jesus that that had been done to him."],[2546.86,"That had been done to him."],[2553.38,"It's it's like just the power of ri uh for people to recover."],[2559.38,"Because he did the opposite for the sick people."],[2563.14,"Well, we sit we read he s he did this filled with the Holy Spirit."],[2567.14,"Meaning the Holy Spirit, God was directing him to do this."],[2571.91,"And that's why God's the one who's doing it."],[2575.51,"Yeah. It's not that St."],[2578.07,"Paul can just walk around striking people blind whenever he feels like it."],[2582.31,"But but it's that it's that God has moved him to say this because God is going to do this."],[2588.79,"Because not only is St."],[2589.75,"Paul concerned about this person and wantinging to to bring him to repentance, God loves this person and wants to br him repentance."],[2592.39,"Christ loves this person"],[2600.19,"There are two people involved because it doesn't say that the proconsul believes because of what he saw."],[2607.39,"So is it like uh for when parents they love their children and they see that they do something wrong and they try to correct them by letting them just telling them exactly what was wrong and to direct them."],[2621.79,"So he that's a kind of love as well."],[2625.25,"If if a parent just let their children go and do whatever they wanted to and didn't care, go play in traffic, you know, go, you wouldn't say they loved their children."],[2636.93,"Yes Okay. You'd say you don't care about your children at all."],[2647.15,"And sometimes you punish them so they will understand their fault."],[2651.95,"And that's what uh is doing here."],[2654.97,"St. Paul's gonna write later what his epistles that God chastens, God punishes every son who he loves."],[2661.21,"Who he loves."],[2662.98,"He disciplines every son who everyone who he loves, he disciplines to try to help them grow and repent and change."],[2671.06,"Yeah, and this is St."],[2672.34,"Paul's also gonna uh say that we should speak the truth in love, and that is one of the most misquoted verses in our because people quote it as if to say, well, you need to speak the truth, but you need to do it in love, meaning you don't always actually speak the truth."],[2688.7,"That's not what St."],[2690.94,"Paul is saying."],[2692.3,"Because you love someone, that's why you tell them the truth."],[2696.54,"That's why you don't lie to them."],[2699.98,"Even if it hurts their feelings right now."],[2703.11,"If it's the truth, it's better that they know the truth."],[2708.04,"And so he's saying if you love someone, you will tell them the truth."],[2712.92,"I think it makes sense."],[2714.84,"Yeah. And so that's what St."],[2718.28,"Paul is doing here."],[2720.04,"It goes against my upbringing."],[2729.76,"Yeah. Yankees were people who did this."],[2733.52,"And that's how we knew we were better than they were."],[2737.84,"Well now, this isn't saying that again, you abuse people."],[2741.3,"Because again, well no, because again, that's the other side."],[2744.64,"We're not talking about parents abusing their children."],[2748.1,"That's the far other that's not love."],[2751.55,"That's doing it without love."],[2754.24,"But, you know, th there's there's an in-between."],[2758.62,"Where you're not lying, but you're also not, you know, try you're not setting out to hurt people."],[2764.32,"You're not like, what, you know, I'm mad at this person, so I'm gonna say something that I know is going to crush them"],[2770.28,"You know, that's you're telling them the truth because you love them."],[2778.28,"And immediately a dark mist fell on him, and he went around seeking someone to lead him by the hand."],[2786.92,"Then the procons consul believed when he saw what had been done, being astonished at the teaching of the Lord."],[2796.28,"Uh we don't read right here whether it works with or Jesus himself."],[2801.92,"Uh-huh. But it it does at least serve to overcome his objections."],[2806.79,"Remember, he was arguing the other side with the pro con."],[2808.55,"Well, this is uh There must be something to what they're saying"],[2810.03,"Well, the Pro Consul sees him get struck blind and says."],[2816.19,"This is kind of a big deal because this guy has the same rank as Pontius Pilate."],[2822.59,"Yeah. Uh and um so here we are."],[2827.71,"Uh we've already reached the point where someone of that importance in the Roman administration has come over."],[2835.61,"And remember, you know, Christ had said that they would testify on his behalf before kings."],[2844.81,"So we're starting to see those kind of things happening."],[2850.33,"Listen next time as Father Stephen DeYoung continues. his study of the scriptures on the Whole Counsel of God"],[2857.65,"Father Stephen's email address is wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."],[2862.93,"That's wholecounsel at ancientfaith.com."]],"o":[{"t":63.64,"lv":2,"h":"Recap: Antioch, persecution, Herod","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Last time: <b>beginnings of the church in Antioch</b>, Paul and Barnabas ministering</li><li class=\"d0\">Further persecution in Jerusalem under <b>Herod</b>: killed <b>James son of Zebedee</b>, St. John's brother</li><li class=\"d1\">Herod then got his comeuppance - <b>eaten by worms</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Pattern: <b>Jewish authorities → Herod → others</b> attack the church</li><li class=\"d1\">every time, church <b>spreads out more and grows more</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Famous saying: \"the <b>blood of the martyrs was the seed of the church</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">as much as they tried to destroy it, it \"blossomed into new life\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Herod's attempt on <b>Peter</b> didn't go as hoped - <b>heavy guard, middle of the barracks</b>, angel let him loose</li><li class=\"d0\">Now attention back to <b>Antioch in Syria</b></li></ul>"},{"t":195.84,"lv":2,"h":"Antioch in Syria vs. other Antiochs","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Stress <b>Antioch in Syria</b> - another <b>Antioch</b> comes up in this very chapter</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Seleucids</b>: dynasty succeeding <b>Alexander the Great</b>, ruled Syria</li><li class=\"d1\">Syria then = what's now <b>Lebanon, Jordan</b>, even <b>Judea</b>, east <b>Turkey</b>, across <b>Iraq, Iran</b>, into <b>Afghanistan</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Six Antiochuses</b> - family name; named cities after themselves, sons, fathers</li><li class=\"d1\">like many <b>Alexandrias</b> after Alexander, many <b>Caesareas</b> after Caesar</li><li class=\"d0\">This Antioch = <b>capital of the eastern Roman Empire</b>; <b>third biggest city</b> after <b>Rome and Alexandria</b></li><li class=\"d1\">other Antiochs are <b>smaller cities</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Opens for questions, then starts <b>Acts 13:1</b></li></ul>"},{"t":322.8,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 13:1 - Prophets and teachers at Antioch","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Church at Antioch: <b>Barnabas</b>, <b>Simeon called Niger</b>, <b>Lucius of Cyrene</b>, <b>Manaen</b> (brought up with <b>Herod the Tetrarch</b>), and <b>Saul</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Herod who just died ≠ <b>Herod the Great</b> (killed infants; died soon after Jesus' birth)</li><li class=\"d1\">Romans unhappy → <b>chopped territory in four</b>; sons inherited pieces (\"not his horses\")</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Tetrarch</b>: <i>arch</i> = ruler (like <b>archbishops</b>), <i>tetra</i> = four → <b>ruler of a fourth</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Manaen</b>: from some <b>wealthy family</b>, connected to the Herods, <b>grew up alongside</b> Herod</li></ul>"},{"t":416.58,"lv":3,"h":"\"Prophets and teachers\"","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Agabus</b> (last time or before) described as a <b>prophet</b> - certain prophets came from Jerusalem</li><li class=\"d0\">Prophet ≠ predictor but <b>teacher in the OT sense</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Barnabas, Saul (= <b>Paul</b>), Simeon, Manaen all described as <b>prophets and teachers</b></li><li class=\"d1\">people <b>preaching the faith, preaching Christ</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: more about the names beyond Barnabas and Paul? - <b>No</b></li></ul>"},{"t":457.5,"lv":3,"h":"Why Luke lists names","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Luke's habit: names pop up - e.g. <b>Simon of Cyrene</b>, whose sons were <b>Rufus</b> - \"why is that?\"</li><li class=\"d1\">real people at time of writing - you could <b>go talk to them: is this true?</b></li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Luke</b> said at the start of his gospel he gathered <b>eyewitnesses' testimony</b>; wasn't with Jesus most of his life</li><li class=\"d1\">names = often <b>people he talked to</b></li><li class=\"d1\">may not have talked to Simon but to <b>his sons</b>: \"we were in Jerusalem... they made our father carry the cross\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Significant people of the church then - Luke reports them as sources</li></ul>"},{"t":557.45,"lv":3,"h":"Luke as historian; the \"we\" sections","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Luke in both gospel and Acts writes in a <b>historical style</b></li><li class=\"d1\">deliberately <b>aping Thucydides</b>, Greek historian</li><li class=\"d1\">emphasis: <b>things that really happened</b> - \"not mythology, not folk tales\"; actual people, events, how it unfolded</li><li class=\"d0\">Soon in Acts: <b>\"they\" becomes \"we\"</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Luke starts <b>traveling with Paul</b> → \"we went here, we traveled there\" = <b>eyewitness</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Earlier parts: he wasn't there, <b>got it from other people</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: wasn't he one of the <b>Emmaus</b> disciples? - <b>traditionally</b> the other unnamed disciple</li><li class=\"d1\">hence \"wasn't there for <b>most</b> of Jesus' earthly life\"; maybe parts, or at the end in <b>Jerusalem</b></li></ul>"},{"t":669.65,"lv":3,"h":"Luke the iconographer and physician","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Tradition: <b>Luke = first iconographer</b> - painted icons of the <b>Theotokos</b></li><li class=\"d1\">one of the people he talked to</li><li class=\"d1\">how he knew <b>Luke 1-2</b>: <b>Archangel Gabriel</b> to Mary, the birth of Jesus</li><li class=\"d0\">Luke was a <b>physician</b> - \"a little different than doctors today\"; helped sick people</li><li class=\"d0\">Those four = primary leaders of the <b>Christian community</b> in Antioch</li><li class=\"d1\">correct to say <b>Christian</b> - in Antioch they had started calling them that; already the title</li></ul>"},{"t":725.77,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 13:2-3 - Barnabas and Saul set apart","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"As they <b>ministered to the Lord</b> and <b>fasted</b>, the Holy Spirit said: <b>separate to me Barnabas and Saul</b> for the work to which I have called them\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Fasted, prayed, <b>laid hands</b> on them, <b>sent them away</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Worshiping <b>together as a community</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Holy Spirit says there is <b>something else</b> Barnabas and Saul need to do</li><li class=\"d1\">there over a <b>year</b> already</li><li class=\"d1\">the other two <b>continue in Antioch</b></li><li class=\"d0\">They fast and pray and <b>set them apart</b> for a mission they <b>don't yet know</b></li><li class=\"d1\">told only: \"let them move on\"</li></ul>"},{"t":774.37,"lv":3,"h":"\"Ministered\" means worship","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Ministered\" - not as we use it: \"I minister to the people of the parish\"</li><li class=\"d0\">What it describes = <b>worship</b></li><li class=\"d1\">word refers to service like that at the <b>Temple</b> - strictly <b>sacrificial worship</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"ministered to the Lord\" = here the <b>Eucharist</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not exactly what we do now - but yes, <b>from early times</b></li><li class=\"d0\">English \"ministered\" translation weaker; <b>\"worshiped\"</b> better - closer to the original languages</li><li class=\"d1\">English further from <b>Greek</b>, definitely from <b>Hebrew and Aramaic</b></li></ul>"},{"t":820.9,"lv":2,"h":"Synagogue Saturday, Eucharist Sunday","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">What we call <b>liturgy</b> was then <b>two separate services</b></li><li class=\"d1\">no \"<b>Judaism over here, Christianity over there</b>\" yet - not two religions</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Saturday</b>: <b>synagogue</b> - Paul goes <b>first to the synagogue on Saturday</b> in every city</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Scripture read and preached</b> - now preaching about <b>Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">reading the <b>OT</b>, NT not yet written</li><li class=\"d1\">this = first part of our liturgy: <b>psalms, Scripture, preaching</b></li><li class=\"d1\">gospel book <b>entrance procession</b> ↔ <b>Torah scroll</b> procession; Orthodox synagogues still do it</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Sunday</b>: just Christians celebrate the <b>Eucharist</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Mid-2nd century</b>: Jews <b>threw Christians out of the synagogue</b>, declared them <b>heretics</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Christians moved the synagogue service <b>to Sunday</b> too</li><li class=\"d1\">why Orthodox do <b>both together</b>: <b>Liturgy of the Word</b>, then Eucharist</li></ul>"},{"t":976.33,"lv":3,"h":"Justin Martyr and 1 Corinthians","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>St. Justin Martyr</b>, mid-2nd century, writing right after the expulsion</li><li class=\"d1\"><b><i>Dialogue with Trypho the Jew</i></b> - Trypho a Jewish acquaintance, friendly debate; mentions Christians expelled</li><li class=\"d1\">also describes <b>Christian worship</b> - \"exactly an outline of what we do on Sunday\"</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Eucharist</b> on the <b>Lord's Day</b>, the day Jesus rose</li><li class=\"d0\">Only a few years before <b>1 Corinthians</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul describes how they do the Eucharist: \"<b>what I received I also hand on to you</b>\"</li><li class=\"d0\">→ Eucharist <b>from the very beginning</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1102.52,"lv":3,"h":"Worship, fasting, the Spirit's call","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ministering = <b>worshiping</b>; fasting together</li><li class=\"d0\">Spirit says Barnabas and Saul have <b>something else to do</b></li><li class=\"d1\">the other two <b>stay in Antioch</b></li><li class=\"d0\">They <b>fast and pray</b> and set them apart for the mission about to begin</li></ul>"},{"t":1158.19,"lv":2,"h":"Saul becomes Paul","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Starts being called <b>Paul</b>, stops being called <b>Saul</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not a formal renaming like <b>Peter</b> (Christ's nickname) or <b>Abram → Abraham</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Same point as <b>John Mark</b>: <b>John = Jonah</b>, Hebrew name; Greeks/Romans couldn't pronounce Hebrew sounds</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Mark</b> = Latin <b>Marcus</b>, not a last name</li><li class=\"d0\">Even <b>Peter</b>: <i>Petros</i> is Greek; actual name <b>Cephas</b>, Aramaic for <b>rock</b></li><li class=\"d1\">Paul calls him <b>Cephas</b> later in his letters - what he was called in Aramaic</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Saul</b>: Paul from the <b>tribe of Benjamin</b> - <b>King Saul's</b> tribe, so probably a popular name there</li><li class=\"d1\">but from <b>Tarsus</b>, major <b>Roman port city</b> - dealings with Greeks and Romans, no Aramaic</li><li class=\"d0\"><b><i>Paulos</i></b> = Greek name; in Eastern Europe <b>Pavel</b>, Arabic <b>Bulos</b>; some names completely different</li><li class=\"d0\">With the Jewish community (Jerusalem, Antioch) → <b>Saul</b>; in Gentile Greek cities → <b>Paul</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Q: was Paul a common Greek name? - not really; likely <b>something close to Saul</b> that gave less trouble</li></ul>"},{"t":1381.35,"lv":3,"h":"Names in other languages - Philistines","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Ancient world full of this - <b>Philistines</b>, where <b>Palestine</b> gets its name</li><li class=\"d1\">from <b>Crete</b>; part of <b>Sea Peoples</b>, tried to invade <b>Egypt</b> and failed</li><li class=\"d1\">set up colonies on the coast: <b>Ashkelon, Ashdod, Gaza</b></li><li class=\"d1\">actual name only consonants <b>P R S T</b> → \"<b>Perest</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">Egyptians \"had a lisp\", couldn't do <b>Rs</b> → <b>Pelest</b> → <b>Philistine / Palestine</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Common names take different forms in each European country</li><li class=\"d0\">Asian friends with American names; <b>Ethiopian</b> parishioner <b>Abanish</b> → everyone called her <b>Abby</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Similar thing with <b>Paul</b></li></ul>"},{"t":1495.65,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 13:4-5 - Seleucia, Cyprus, Salamis","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Sent by the <b>Holy Spirit</b> → down to <b>Seleucia</b>, sailed to <b>Cyprus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">At <b>Salamis</b> preached the word of God <b>in the synagogues of the Jews</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>John</b> as assistant = <b>John Mark</b>, author of Mark's gospel</li><li class=\"d1\">went back to Antioch with them last time; now brought along - \"still quite young\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Why <b>Cyprus first</b>: <b>Barnabas is from there</b></li><li class=\"d1\">had <b>estates</b>, converted in Jerusalem, <b>sold them</b> and stayed</li><li class=\"d1\">\"knows lots of people there\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"<b>Preached the word</b>\" - word isn't the Bible yet: <b>Word = Jesus, Christ</b></li><li class=\"d1\">proclaim in synagogues: the <b>Messiah has come, Jesus of Nazareth</b>, what he did, who he is</li><li class=\"d0\">Start in <b>Salamis</b>, a major <b>port city</b> of Cyprus, then work outward</li></ul>"},{"t":1606.93,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 13:6 - The sorcerer Bar-Jesus at Paphos","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Through the island to <b>Paphos</b>: a <b>sorcerer, false prophet, a Jew named Bar-Jesus</b></li><li class=\"d0\">\"Bar Jesus\" = poor transliteration</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Bar</b> = \"<b>son of</b>\" in Aramaic; <b>Joshua / Yeshua</b> - his father's name</li><li class=\"d1\">common OT name; <b>no relation</b> to Jesus of Nazareth</li><li class=\"d0\">Notice: the sorcerer is <b>Jewish</b> - not a Gentile doing paganism</li><li class=\"d0\">Going city to city around Cyprus</li></ul>"},{"t":1674.79,"lv":2,"h":"Judaism then wasn't rabbinic Judaism","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">We assume <b>rabbinic Judaism</b> is what Judaism always was - \"<b>literally a different religion</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">comes into being <b>5th-6th centuries</b> as the <b>Talmud</b> is finalized</li><li class=\"d0\">Excavated <b>first-century synagogues in Galilee</b> (five)</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>zodiacs</b> in floor mosaics; one with <b>Medusa heads</b> outside to scare off evil spirits</li><li class=\"d1\">even near Jerusalem: lots of <b>pagan superstitions</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Goes back to the <b>OT prophets</b> always worried about pagan practices - a \"smooshing\"</li><li class=\"d0\">The further from Jerusalem, <b>less pure</b></li><li class=\"d1\">many never saw Jerusalem or took part in a <b>sacrifice</b> unless wealthy - travel expensive</li><li class=\"d1\">only religion: <b>Saturday synagogue prayer</b>; lived in a <b>pagan world</b> six days, some seven</li><li class=\"d0\">Parallel for us: Sunday Christian, rest of the week \"do what I gotta do\" - <b>a temptation for us too</b>; many gave in</li></ul>"},{"t":1858.47,"lv":3,"h":"Sorcery as a living","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Bar-Jesus gave in - <b>a way to make a living</b>, not just odd religion</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>astrology</b>, interpreting <b>dreams and omens</b>, <b>potions</b> for love or energy</li><li class=\"d1\">\"<b>snake oil salesmen</b>\" - not new</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: common in Judaism? - <b>not</b>, but very common in the <b>pagan world</b></li><li class=\"d0\">A Jewish person \"blended into his pagan environment\"</li><li class=\"d1\">may or may not still show at synagogue Saturday; rest of week lived as a pagan</li><li class=\"d0\">Aside: \"poor Jesus\" (his name)</li></ul>"},{"t":1929.39,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 13:7 - Proconsul Sergius Paulus","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">With the <b>proconsul, Sergius Paulus, an intelligent man</b> - called for Barnabas and Saul and <b>sought to hear the word of God</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Proconsul</b> = local representative of the <b>Roman government</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Connection through the sorcerer; probably he <b>showed up at a synagogue</b> now and then</li><li class=\"d1\">they're preaching in synagogues - likely where he met them (\"don't know for sure\")</li><li class=\"d0\">He tells the proconsul: two going around with <b>this new thing</b> - \"you ought to hear it\"</li></ul>"},{"t":1986.17,"lv":2,"h":"Oratory as entertainment","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Seems strange - but <b>no TV, smartphones, YouTube, newspapers</b>; <b>few books</b>, no personal libraries</li><li class=\"d1\">Alexandria maybe, if wealthy</li><li class=\"d0\">Entertainment: <b>plays, athletic contests, gladiatorial games, orations</b></li><li class=\"d1\">big emphasis on <b>speaking</b>; schools of <b>rhetoric</b>; <b>Aristotle</b> treatises</li><li class=\"d1\">good speaker → didn't matter what about; <b>entertaining</b>, people came</li><li class=\"d0\">So Bar-Jesus not a sincere convert-seeker: \"these guys are <b>impressive speakers</b>, you might find it <b>diverting</b>\"</li><li class=\"d1\">like recommending a <b>TV show</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Lasted a long time - radio destroyed it; <b>19th-century lecturers</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>George Whitefield</b> - preached for hours</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Ben Franklin's autobiography</b>: wasn't paying attention to content; <b>paced off</b> how far away he could still hear - <b>a couple of blocks</b></li><li class=\"d0\">We're \"disassociated from that now\"</li></ul>"},{"t":2182.66,"lv":3,"h":"\"An intelligent man\"; Elymas","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\"><b>Intelligent</b> = <b>educated</b>, <b>curious</b> about this kind of thing</li><li class=\"d1\">many people aren't curious; he was</li><li class=\"d0\">Sorcerer = <b>Bar-Jesus</b> and <b>Elymas</b> same person</li><li class=\"d1\"><b>Elymas</b> = \"the sorcerer\" (a translation of the name)</li><li class=\"d1\">Bar-Jesus = \"son of his dad\"; <b>Elymas</b> = what he'd have called himself</li><li class=\"d0\">He <b>isn't sold</b> - as interesting as he found Paul and Barnabas, <b>not converting</b></li><li class=\"d0\">When he sees the proconsul might be <b>swayed</b>, he <b>argues the counterpoint</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2286.81,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 13:9-11 - Paul rebukes Elymas","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Then <b>Saul, who also is called Paul</b>\" - first time we're told this</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Filled with the Holy Spirit</b>, looks intently: \"O full of all <b>deceit and fraud</b>, <b>son of the devil</b>, <b>enemy of all righteousness</b>, will you not cease <b>perverting the straight ways of the Lord</b>?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"The <b>hand of the Lord is upon you</b>, you shall be <b>blind</b>, not seeing the sun for a time\"</li><li class=\"d0\">\"A little harsh\"</li><li class=\"d1\">same with <b>Christ and the Pharisees</b> in <b>John</b>: \"<b>brood of vipers</b>, hypocrites, woe to you\" - \"really unchrist-like?\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Because of <b>modern culture</b>: very concerned about <b>offending people</b>, easily offended</li></ul>"},{"t":2368.48,"lv":2,"h":"Truth over avoiding offense","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Then: <b>more concerned with telling the truth</b> than offending</li><li class=\"d1\">what can cause someone to <b>change their mind, repent</b> = <b>the truth</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Letting someone \"<b>wander off and go to hell</b>\" isn't loving</li><li class=\"d0\">If someone is doing evil, destroying themselves spiritually → most loving thing is <b>tell them</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"you're lying to yourself... <b>repent and do good</b>\" = best thing that can happen to them</li><li class=\"d0\">So Paul, <b>albeit harshly</b> (sometimes harshness is what you need), is trying to get him to <b>repent and see the truth</b></li><li class=\"d1\">\"if he didn't love him, he wouldn't have said anything\"; would've ignored him</li></ul>"},{"t":2477.21,"lv":3,"h":"The blindness as a sign - Paul's own story","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Q: blindness not permanent? - <b>\"for a time\"</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul gives a <b>sign</b>: \"you'll be blind a while\" so you know it's <b>true</b></li><li class=\"d1\">not just a personal disagreement</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Exactly what happened to Paul</b> a few chapters ago</li><li class=\"d1\">going the wrong way: <b>persecuting Christians</b>, opposing Christ</li><li class=\"d1\">Christ revealed himself, knocked him off, <b>struck blind</b> → <b>turned his life around</b></li><li class=\"d0\">→ Paul tries to do for <b>Elymas</b> what was done for him</li><li class=\"d0\">Said <b>filled with the Holy Spirit</b> = <b>God directing</b> it; God is the one doing it</li><li class=\"d1\">not that Paul can <b>strike people blind</b> whenever he likes</li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Christ loves this person</b> and wants him to repent too</li></ul>"},{"t":2600.19,"lv":2,"h":"Parental correction; speaking the truth in love","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">Doesn't say the proconsul believes <b>because of</b> the blinding yet - two people involved</li><li class=\"d0\">Q: like parents correcting a child? - yes, <b>a kind of love</b></li><li class=\"d1\">parent who lets child <b>play in traffic</b> doesn't love; sometimes <b>punish so they understand</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Paul later writes: <b>God chastens every son he loves</b> - disciplines to help them <b>grow, repent, change</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>\"Speak the truth in love\"</b> - most misquoted: \"truth, but <b>in love</b>\" = not always actually speak it</li><li class=\"d1\">Paul means: <b>because you love</b> someone you tell them the truth; <b>you don't lie to them</b>, even if it hurts feelings now</li><li class=\"d0\">Aside: \"goes against my upbringing\" - Yankees \"were people who did this\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Not <b>abuse</b> - parents abusing children is the <b>other extreme</b>, \"not love\"</li><li class=\"d0\">In-between: not lying, also <b>not setting out to hurt</b>; not \"I'm mad, I'll say what I know will crush them\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Telling the truth <b>because you love them</b></li></ul>"},{"t":2778.28,"lv":2,"h":"Acts 13:11-12 - A dark mist; the proconsul believes","b":"<ul><li class=\"d0\">\"Immediately a <b>dark mist</b> fell on him\" - went around seeking someone to <b>lead him by the hand</b></li><li class=\"d0\"><b>Proconsul believed</b> when he saw what was done, <b>astonished at the teaching of the Lord</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Doesn't say whether it works with <b>Elymas</b> or Jesus himself</li><li class=\"d1\">at least <b>overcomes his objections</b> - Elymas had argued the other side</li><li class=\"d0\">Proconsul sees him struck blind: \"there must be something to what they're saying\"</li><li class=\"d0\">Big deal: same <b>rank as Pontius Pilate</b></li><li class=\"d0\">Already someone of that importance in <b>Roman administration</b> has come over</li><li class=\"d0\">Christ said they would <b>testify before kings</b> → \"we're starting to see those kind of things\"</li></ul>"}]});
